Date: Sun, 18 Apr 93 04:24:45 EDT
From: carlf@ai.mit.edu (Carl Feynman)
Subject: Big Brother: Easy to Break

   From: Harry Shapiro <habs@panix.com>
   Date: Sat, 17 Apr 1993 09:39:44 -0400 (EDT)

   I recall having a conversation at the Boston ExI meeting, talking
   about super computers and how fast they could break small keys
   (like 80  bits).

   I think it was carlf, and I don't recall the exact estimate given,
   it I think it was on the order of several hours to several days for
   a fast connection machine class super computer to break such a key
   by brute force.

I was talking about the 56-bit keys used for DES.  80 bits is long
enough to preclude reasonable solution times for simple exhausive
search.  Here's how I estimate this.  Assume that a Clipper uses an
eight-byte block, and can handle 16Kbytes/sec.  That means a single
chip decryption engine can test 2000 keys/sec.  If Clipper costs $10,
and the NSA spends $10^8 on an exhausive cracking machine, they can
test 2x10^11 keys/sec.  Unfortunately, testing all 2^80=10^24 keys
will take 5x10^12 sec, which is hundreds of years.  

However, if we can build a mechanical nanocomputer like that proposed
by Drexler, we will be able to test 10^24 keys at a cost of 10,000
megajoules, or $300 at current electric rates.

--carlf

